    "partNumber": 4,
    "transcript": "Test 3 Part 4. Antikythera mechanism. cargo (31); rock (32); inscriptions (33); cracks (34); metal (35); wood (36); handle (37); eclipse (38); calendar (39); clocks (40).",
    "formContent": "<div class=\"ielts-form-container\"><div class=\"form-section\"><div class=\"form-title\" style=\"text-align:center;font-weight:700;\">The world&rsquo;s oldest mechanical computer: the Antikythera mechanism</div><p><strong>Discovery</strong></p><p>It was part of a ship&rsquo;s <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"31\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> , found in the sea near Antikythera, in Greece.</p><p>It was wrongly thought to be a piece of <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"32\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"></p><p>It was later found to be a mechanism that had broken into pieces.</p><p><strong>Equipment used for analysis of the mechanism</strong></p><ul style=\"line-height:2;\"><li>&ldquo;Dome&rdquo; - produces photographs which make the <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"33\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> clearer</li><li>&ldquo;BladeRunner&rdquo; - produces X-prays</li><li>originally used to identify <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"34\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> in engines</li></ul><p><strong>Description</strong></p><p>The mechanism consisted of:</p><ul style=\"line-height:2;\"><li>30 or more gear wheels made of <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"35\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"></li><li>models of the sun, moon and planets</li><li>a framework made of <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"36\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"></li></ul><p><strong>How the mechanism was used</strong></p><p>The operator turned a <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"37\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> to move the gear wheels.</p><p>The sun, moon and planets could be moved into their correct positions for any date.</p><p>Most surprisingly, the mechanism could calculate when an <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"38\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> would occur.</p><p>It may have been used as a <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"39\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> when planning festivals.</p><p><strong>Later use of similar technology</strong></p><p>13th &ndash; 14th centuries: used for making <input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" class=\"gap-input\" data-q=\"40\" style=\"width:150px;text-align:center;\"> in Western Europe.</p></div></div>",
